%N A052434 Nearest integer to R(n) - pi(n), where R(x) is Riemann's R function.
%C A052434 From _Ali Ramadhan_, Oct 14 2026: (Start)
%C A052434 The Riemann's R function R(n) = Sum_{k>=1} mu(k)*li(n^(1/k))/k.
%C A052434 Equivalently, R(x) = 1 + Sum_{k>=1} log(x)^k/(k*k!*zeta(k+1)) (Gram's series); this is RiemannR in Mathematica. (End)
%H A052434 Harry J. Smith, <a href="/A052434/b052434.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 2..10000</a>
%H A052434 H. J. Smith, <a href="http://harry-j-smith-memorial.com/download.html#XPCalc">XPCalc - Extra Precision Floating-Point Calculator</a> [Broken link]
%H A052434 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/PrimeCountingFunction.html">Prime Counting Function</a>.
%H A052434 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/RiemannPrimeCountingFunction.html">Riemann Prime Counting Function</a>.
%e A052434 a(13) = 0 because R(13) = 5.504 and pi(13) = 6.
%t A052434 A052434[n_] := Floor[RiemannR[n] - PrimePi[n] + 1/2];
%t A052434 Array[A052434, 100, 2] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Oct 22 2026 *)
%o A052434 (PARI) R(x) = my(L=log(x), t=1., s=1.); for(k=1, oo, t *= L/k; my(u = t/(k*zeta(k+1))); s += u; if(u < 1e-30*s, break)); s
%o A052434 a(n) = round(R(n) - primepi(n)) \\ _Ali Ramadhan_, Oct 14 2026
%o A052434 (Julia)
%o A052434 using Primes, SpecialFunctions
%o A052434 function R(x)  # Riemann's R function, from Gram's series
%o A052434     L = log(x)
%o A052434     s = t = 1.0
%o A052434     k = 0
%o A052434     while t > 1e-17 * s
%o A052434         k += 1
%o A052434         t *= L / k
%o A052434         s += t / (k * zeta(k + 1))
%o A052434     end
%o A052434     return s
%o A052434 end
%o A052434 a(n) = round(Int, R(n) - length(primes(n)))  # _Ali Ramadhan_, Oct 14 2026
%Y A052434 Cf. A052435, A057794, A399820 (partial sums).
%K A052434 sign,changed
%O A052434 2,108
%A A052434 _Eric W. Weisstein_
%E A052434 Corrected 6 terms, a(2), a(7), a(10), a(13), a(20) and a(48). Each was made 1 larger. Also gave an example for a(13) and a program for computing a(n). - _Harry J. Smith_, Dec 31 2008
